---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const webCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · HTML — the structure', note: 'Elements with meaning: <h1> a heading, <p> a paragraph, <img> an image. The browser builds a tree (the DOM) from it. Semantic HTML — choosing tags for meaning — is what makes pages accessible and findable.' },
    { math: '2 · CSS — the appearance', note: 'Rules that select elements and style them: colours, layout, fonts. The box model (content, padding, border, margin) and flexbox/grid cover most of layout work.' },
    { math: '3 · JavaScript — the behaviour', note: 'The only language browsers execute natively. It reacts to clicks, fetches data, and rewrites the page live — single-threaded and event-driven, which shapes everything about how web code is written.' },
    { math: '4 · The round trip', note: 'Browser requests HTML → builds DOM → applies CSS → runs JS → fetches more data (fetch()) → updates the DOM. Modern frameworks (React, Vue) just automate that last loop.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/web-development-html-css-js">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The web runs on three languages with a clean division of labour: <strong>HTML is the
    structure, CSS the appearance, JavaScript the behaviour</strong>. This site you're reading is
    exactly that stack. Learn the trio properly and you can build for every device on Earth —
    the browser is the most widely deployed runtime in history, no install required.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the page pipeline — from file to pixels" widget="equationStep" config={webCfg} />

  <h2>Principles that separate good web work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Progressive enhancement:</strong> HTML that works alone, CSS that improves it, JS that upgrades it further. If JavaScript fails, the content still exists — that resilience is why the web outlived flashier rivals (RIP Flash, 2020).</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility is not optional:</strong> alt text, keyboard navigation, contrast, semantic tags. Screen-reader users, slow connections and search engines all reward the same discipline.</li>
    <li><strong>Responsive design:</strong> one page, many screens — fluid layouts and media queries instead of separate mobile sites. Mobile is most of the traffic.</li>
    <li><strong>Performance budget:</strong> every kilobyte costs someone's data plan. Static generation (like this atlas), caching and lazy loading are how the fastest sites stay fast.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MDN Web Docs; Berners-Lee, "Information Management: A Proposal" (1989); W3C accessibility guidelines (WCAG).</p>
</TopicPage>
